<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Woman of 34 with six weeks of fluctuating ptosis, diplopia and fatigable weakness of the arms, neck, chewing and voice, while recovering from a severe car crash last year: suspected generalized myasthenia gravis. Please confirm the diagnosis and advise on treatment, thymectomy and safety. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Do you agree with the diagnosis, and are MuSK antibodies or single-fiber EMG needed?</item><item>What pyridostigmine dose, and how should immunotherapy begin?</item><item>Which steroid-sparing agent suits a woman of 34 with polycystic kidney disease, and what screening is needed first?</item><item>Should she have a thymectomy, and when, so soon after major trauma?</item><item>Is there a place now for newer targeted therapies such as efgartigimod or eculizumab?</item><item>What should she and primary care watch for: crisis warning signs, medicines to avoid, and any kidney considerations?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Mankiller is seen at the request of Dr. Hawkfeather-Lindqvist for six weeks of drooping eyelids, double vision by evening, tiring of the arms when brushing her hair, a tired jaw while eating, and a nasal voice after long conversations. Pyridostigmine 30 mg three times daily has helped her eyelids for a few hours after each dose, with mild cramps. She has had no breathlessness, choking or weak cough. She is recovering from a head-on car crash last year with fractures of both legs, ribs and face and many operations, and walks with a cane on rough ground. She has hereditary polycystic kidney disease with normal kidney function. She works for the Cherokee Nation and lives in rural Adair County. She wants to understand each treatment before agreeing to it, and to have blood tests near home. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Wilma Pearl Mankiller was born on November 18, 1945, in Tahlequah, Oklahoma, the capital of the Cherokee Nation, the sixth of eleven children; her father was a full-blood Cherokee. A Cherokee Nation release gave her birthplace as the W.W. Hastings Indian Hospital in Tahlequah; the Oklahoma Hall of Fame says she was born at Rock Mountain in Adair County. [D]</item><item>As a child she moved with her family from Mankiller Flats in Adair County to San Francisco under the Bureau of Indian Affairs relocation program (1956 or 1957 by different accounts); she later described the move as her own little Trail of Tears. [D]</item><item>The occupation of Alcatraz Island in 1969 and her work with American Indian communities in California in the 1970s set the course of her activism. [D]</item><item>She returned to eastern Oklahoma with her two daughters in the later 1970s (1976 or 1977 by different accounts), worked for the Cherokee Nation, and lived on her grandfather&apos;s allotment, Mankiller Flats, in Adair County. [D]</item><item>In 1979 she was nearly killed in a head-on car crash on a curve, in which the driver of the other car, one of her best friends, died. She needed about 17 operations; accounts say her leg was barely saved from amputation and that she was told she might never walk again. The Oklahoma Hall of Fame dates the crash to 1978 and describes shattered legs, broken ribs and a crushed face. [D]</item><item>Her recovery was made harder by the emotional trauma of her friend&apos;s death in the crash. [D]</item></list><paragraph>8 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

